            <ArticleTitle>Resolution of Comorbidities and Impact on Longevity Following Bariatric and Metabolic Surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>717-32</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.suc.2016.03.007</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0039-6109(16)30007-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Bariatric surgery is the most effective and durable treatment of severe obesity. In addition to weight loss, these operations result in significant improvement or resolution of many obesity-related comorbid diseases. There are now numerous studies demonstrating that bariatric surgery decreases all-cause mortality long-term compared with cohorts of patients who did not undergo surgery. Decreases in cancer, diabetes, and cardiovascular-related mortality are major contributors to this overall effect on life expectancy after bariatric surgery.</AbstractText>
